{
  "area": "combat-action-table",
  "description": "How a world’s authored rows become combat actions (US-3/US-4 of 120-combat-and-stamina). `actions` is the SHARED action block (`systems.actions`) and `combat` is combat’s columns on those same rows (`WorldIR.combat.actions`) — never a second table, the way `item_damage/2` extends the item block. `expected.rows` is CombatActionTable.loadFromIR’s result in load order, `expected.facts` is what the table publishes back into the KB (`action_range/2`, and deliberately nothing else — §11 and §12 of docs/mechanic-predicates.md keep rolls and combat-loop tuning out of the vocabulary), and `projectiles`/`defensive` are the two shape queries. The precedence pinned here is combat column → shared row → unset, where a 0 on the shared row is unset and a 0 in a column is authored. Regenerate with `npm run combat-corpus`.",
  "cases": [
    {
      "name": "a-combat-category-row-alone-is-a-complete-melee-action",
      "note": "A world that authored no combat columns at all still has a table: an action the creator filed under `combat` with a range and an energy cost IS a melee attack. The row’s `range` becomes the action’s reach and its `energyCost` becomes the shared meter’s price — one action table, and combat reads the columns that already exist.",
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "swing",
          "category": "combat",
          "range": 2,
          "energyCost": 8
        },
        {
          "id": "haul_water",
          "category": "chore",
          "range": 0,
          "energyCost": 12
        }
      ],
      "combat": null,
      "expected": {
        "loaded": 1,
        "rows": [
          {
            "id": "swing",
            "range": 2,
            "staminaCost": 8
          }
        ],
        "facts": [
          {
            "predicate": "action_range",
            "args": [
              "swing",
              2
            ]
          }
        ],
        "projectiles": [],
        "defensive": []
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "combat-columns-win-over-the-shared-row",
      "note": "The precedence, which is the thing four engines will each get subtly wrong: combat column → shared row → (at resolution) the weapon’s authored stat → the tuning. Here both rows author a range and a cost and the columns override both.",
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "swing",
          "category": "combat",
          "range": 2,
          "energyCost": 8
        }
      ],
      "combat": {
        "style": "melee",
        "settings": {
          "baseDamage": 10,
          "damageVariance": 0.2,
          "criticalChance": 0.15,
          "criticalMultiplier": 2,
          "blockReduction": 0.5,
          "dodgeChance": 0.05,
          "attackCooldown": 1,
          "comboWindowMs": 500,
          "maxComboLength": 3
        },
        "actions": [
          {
            "id": "swing",
            "damage": 15,
            "damageType": "slashing",
            "range": 3,
            "staminaCost": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      "expected": {
        "loaded": 1,
        "rows": [
          {
            "id": "swing",
            "damage": 15,
            "damageType": "slashing",
            "range": 3,
            "staminaCost": 11
          }
        ],
        "facts": [
          {
            "predicate": "action_range",
            "args": [
              "swing",
              3
            ]
          }
        ],
        "projectiles": [],
        "defensive": []
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "a-zero-on-the-shared-row-reads-as-unset",
      "note": "`ActionIR.range` and `ActionIR.energyCost` default to 0, not to absent. Reading a literal 0 as a reach would silently defeat `item_range/2` — every weapon would be out of reach at any separation above zero — so a 0 leaves the field off the row and the weapon’s stat answers. A column of 0 is different: that one was authored, and it stands.",
      "actions": [
        {
          "id": "swing",
          "category": "combat",
          "range": 0,
          "energyCost": 0
        }
      ],
      "combat": null,
      "expected": {
        "loaded": 1,
        "rows": [
          {
            "id": "swing"
          }
        ],
        "facts": [],
        "projectiles": [],
        "defensive": []
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "a-projectile-row-and-a-defensive-row-are-the-same-type-as-a-swing",
      "note": "THE US-3 case, pinned. Three rows, one type: the bolt differs by `delivery` and `range`, the sidestep by a `defense` profile and no damage. There is no discriminator and no second shape — which is what makes adding a weapon or a dodge variant a row of authored content rather than a `switch` a native port has to grow a case in.",
      "actions": [
